{"tool_name":"rboanalyzer","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"rboAnalyzer","home":"http://rboanalyzer.elixir-czech.cz/","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"A tool for analyzing BLAST search output for RNA sequences.","addition_date":"2019-10-26T15:35:08Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:21:20.325954Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application"],"version":["0.1.4"],"collections":["Czech Republic","ELIXIR-CZ","MBU AV CR"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:rboAnalyzer","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"vohr@biomed.cas.cz","name":"Ji\u0159\u00ed Vohradsk\u00fd","note":"Head of Laboratory of Bioinformatics at IMG AS CR","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"https://mbucas.cz/en/research/biology-of-the-cell-and-bioinformatics/laboratory-of-bioinformatics/"},{"name":"Martin Modr\u00e1k","note":"Researcher at Laboratory of Bioinformatics at IMG AS CR","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0002-8886-7797","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Marek Schwarz","note":"Student at Laboratory of Bioinformatics at IMG AS CR","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["Command-line options"],"url":"https://github.com/cas-bioinf/rboAnalyzer#basic-usage"},{"type":["Installation instructions"],"url":"https://github.com/cas-bioinf/rboAnalyzer#install-from-source"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["schwarzm"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"cmd":"rboAnalyzer -in BLAST_OUTPUT.txt -db USED_DATABASE_PATH -q BLAST_QUERY.fasta","input":[{"data":{"term":"RNA sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3495"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}],"note":"Run analysis on BLAST_QUERY sequence over USED_DATABASE with output from BLAST itself","operation":[{"term":"Nucleic acid sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2478"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2955"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"},{"term":"HTML","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2331"}]}]}],"maturity":"Emerging","name":"rboAnalyzer","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"ELIXIR-CZ","publication":[{"doi":"10.3389/fgene.2020.00675","metadata":{"abstract":"Searching for similar sequences in a database via BLAST or a similar tool is one of the most common bioinformatics tasks applied in general, and to non-coding RNAs in particular. However, the results of the search might be difficult to interpret due to the presence of partial matches to the database subject sequences. Here, we present rboAnalyzer \u2013 a tool that helps with interpreting sequence search result by (1) extending partial matches into plausible full-length subject sequences, (2) predicting homology of RNAs represented by full-length subject sequences to the query RNA, (3) pooling information across homologous RNAs found in the search results and public databases such as Rfam to predict more reliable secondary structures for all matches, and (4) contextualizing the matches by providing the prediction results and other relevant information in a rich graphical output. Using predicted full-length matches improves secondary structure prediction and makes rboAnalyzer robust with regards to identification of homology. The output of the tool should help the user to reliably characterize non-coding RNAs in BLAST output. The usefulness of the rboAnalyzer and its ability to correctly extend partial matches to full-length is demonstrated on known homologous RNAs. To allow the user to use custom databases and search options, rboAnalyzer accepts any search results as a text file in the BLAST format. The main output is an interactive HTML page displaying the computed characteristics and other context of the matches. The output can also be exported in an appropriate sequence and/or secondary structure formats.","authors":[{"name":"Modrak M."},{"name":"Panek J."},{"name":"Schwarz M."},{"name":"Vohradsky J."}],"citationCount":1,"date":"2020-07-28T00:00:00Z","journal":"Frontiers in Genetics","title":"rboAnalyzer: A Software to Improve Characterization of Non-coding RNAs From Sequence Database Search Output"},"pmcid":"PMC7401326","pmid":"32849767","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btab073","metadata":{"abstract":"We present a web service for improving characterization of non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs) from NCBI BLAST outputs, based on a command-line application rboAnalyzer. Briefly, the application extends subject sequences of selected high scoring pairs (HSPs) in BLAST output to their plausible full length, and predicts their homology and secondary structures. The aim of the application is to aid to characterize subject RNAs in HSPs that come uncharacterized in BLAST output. The main advantages of the web-server are easy use and interactive analysis with search, filtering and data export options.","authors":[{"name":"Panek J."},{"name":"Schwarz M."},{"name":"Vohradsky J."}],"citationCount":1,"date":"2021-09-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"rboAnalyzer webserver: Web service for non-coding RNA characterization from NCBI BLAST output"},"pmid":"33523120","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Functional, regulatory and non-coding RNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0659"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"rboAnalyzer","home":"https://bio.tools/rboAnalyzer","license":"GPL-2.0","version":"0.1.4","summary":"A tool for analyzing BLAST search output for RNA sequences.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:rboanalyzer/cmd/rboanalyzer.elixir-czech.cz","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"e